hypotheses.statement:
Suppose that $S$ is a smooth connected projective complex surface and that there are rational Hodge maps $$h_i:U(v_i)\longrightarrow H^1(S,\mathbb{Q}),\qquad 1\leq i\leq4,$$ and nonzero vectors $\alpha_i\in U(v_i)_{\lambda_i}\subset U(v_i)_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$ such that $$\label{eq:surface-period}
 \int_S h_1(\alpha_1)\smile h_2(\alpha_2)
             \smile h_3(\alpha_3)\smile h_4(\alpha_4)\ne0.$$

Suppose that, for each $1\leq i\leq4$, a class $\eta_i\in H^1(S,\mathbb{C})$ belongs to $$\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{C}}
 \{h(U(v_i)_{\lambda_i}):
       h\in\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}_{\mathrm{Hdg}}(U(v_i),H^1(S,\mathbb{Q}))\}$$ and that $$\int_S\eta_1\smile\eta_2\smile\eta_3\smile\eta_4\ne0.$$

outputs.exact_statement:
For complex abelian varieties $A$ and $B$, pullback induces an isomorphism $$\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}(A,B)\otimes_{\mathbb{Z}}\mathbb{Q}
 \ \simeq\
 \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}_{\mathrm{Hdg}}\bigl(H^1(B,\mathbb{Q}),H^1(A,\mathbb{Q})\bigr).$$ If $S$ is a smooth connected projective complex variety and $h:H^1(B,\mathbb{Q})\to H^1(S,\mathbb{Q})$ is a rational Hodge map, then there are an integer $m\geq1$ and a morphism $f:S\to B$ such that $f^*=mh$ on $H^1(B,\mathbb{Q})$.

Let $E$ be a Galois CM field with conjugation $c$, and let $v_1,v_2,v_3,v_4:G_E\to\{1,-1\}$ be odd sign functions satisfying $v_1+v_2=v_3+v_4$. Put $\lambda=(1,1,c,c)$. Suppose that $S$ is a smooth connected projective complex surface and that there are rational Hodge maps $$h_i:U(v_i)\longrightarrow H^1(S,\mathbb{Q}),\qquad 1\leq i\leq4,$$ and nonzero vectors $\alpha_i\in U(v_i)_{\lambda_i}\subset U(v_i)_{\overline{\mathbb{Q}}}$ such that $$\label{eq:surface-period}
 \int_S h_1(\alpha_1)\smile h_2(\alpha_2)
             \smile h_3(\alpha_3)\smile h_4(\alpha_4)\ne0.$$ Here each $h_i$ is extended to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-coefficients. Then the line in Equation <a href="#eq:switch-line" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:switch-line">[eq:switch-line]</a> is algebraic.

Let $E$, $v_1,\ldots,v_4$, and $\lambda=(1,1,c,c)$ be as in Proposition <a href="#prop:surface-switch" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:surface-switch">9</a>, and let $S$ be a smooth connected projective complex surface. Suppose that, for each $1\leq i\leq4$, a class $\eta_i\in H^1(S,\mathbb{C})$ belongs to $$\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{C}}
 \{h(U(v_i)_{\lambda_i}):
       h\in\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}_{\mathrm{Hdg}}(U(v_i),H^1(S,\mathbb{Q}))\}$$ and that $$\int_S\eta_1\smile\eta_2\smile\eta_3\smile\eta_4\ne0.$$ Then the line in Equation <a href="#eq:switch-line" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:switch-line">[eq:switch-line]</a> is algebraic.

The Hermitian forms on the lines $W_i$ can be chosen over $E/F$ so that $\mathcal F_P$ is a holomorphic positive Lagrangian for $\psi_P$, equivariantly for $G(F\otimes_\mathbb{Q}\mathbb{R})$. Its determinant and an equivariant square root have the form $$\label{eq:theta-halfdet}
 \det\mathcal F_P\simeq L^{2n}\otimes C_P,\qquad
 (\det\mathcal F_P)^{1/2}\simeq L^n\otimes C_P^{1/2},$$ where $C_P$ is a constant complex line on which $G(F\otimes_\mathbb{Q}\mathbb{R})$ acts trivially.

Let $g\in\mathop{\mathrm{Sp}}(\mathbb V_P)(F)$. At each real place write $$g=\begin{pmatrix}A&B\\C&D_0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 J=CZ+D_0,\qquad gZ=(AZ+B)J^{-1}.$$ Choose an infinite metaplectic lift with factor $j(g,Z)$ satisfying $j(g,Z)^2=\prod_{\tau\in\Sigma}\det J_\tau$, and let $\widetilde g_f$ be its paired finite lift. Then $$\label{eq:theta-jacobi-law}
 \vartheta_{\omega_f(\widetilde g_f)\phi}(gZ,J^{-t}z)
  =j(g,Z)\exp\!\left(\pi i\sum_{\tau\in\Sigma}
             z_\tau^tJ_\tau^{-1}C_\tau z_\tau\right)
             \vartheta_\phi(Z,z).$$ The completed coefficients satisfy $$\label{eq:theta-taylor-law}
 T_{k,\omega_f(\widetilde g_f)\phi}(gZ)
   =j(g,Z)\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}^k(J)\,T_{k,\phi}(Z).$$ They are thus tensors valued in $$(\det\mathcal F_P)^{1/2}\otimes\mathop{\mathrm{Sym}}^k\mathcal F_P.$$ In particular the positive Lagrangian, not its dual, occurs in the symmetric factor. The tensor $T_{1,\phi}$ is holomorphic.

Suppose $d_1+d_2=d_3+d_4$, with $d_i(\mu)=1$ for all $i$. Start with $W=W_1\perp W_2$ as above, let $t_0$ be its compact-place data, and choose a total input $\phi$ for which $\xi=\Xi_W(\phi;t_0)$ is a nonzero holomorphic top form on a sufficiently deep compact principal quotient $X$. There are rational orthogonal decompositions $W=W_3\perp W_4$ with line signs $d_3,d_4$ whose total compact-place positive Lagrangians are arbitrarily close to those at $t_0$. The total active positive Lagrangian is exactly unchanged.*


*For a sufficiently close rational choice $t'$, the form $\xi'=\Xi_W(\phi;t')$ is holomorphic on the same quotient $X$, with the same total finite input, and $$\label{eq:theta-nonzero-pairing}
 \int_X\xi\wedge\overline{\xi'}\ne0.$$ Both endpoints admit the wedge expansions of Proposition <a href="#prop:theta-top" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="prop:theta-top">16</a> on a further common principal cover, and their pairing remains nonzero there.

Let $v:G_E\to\{1,-1\}$ be odd with $v(1)=1$, and use the multiplicity-line theta construction prescribed by $d(\tau)=v(\tau^{-1})$. Given a finite collection of its finite adelic Schwartz inputs, there is a common sufficiently deep principal ball quotient $S_0=\Gamma\backslash D$ on which every associated theta one-form class belongs to $$\label{eq:cm-extraction-span}
\operatorname{span}_{\mathbb{C}}\left\{
f\bigl(U(v)_1\bigr):
f\in\mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}_{\mathrm{Hdg}}\bigl(U(v),H^1(S_0,\mathbb{Q})\bigr)
\right\}.$$ The maps in this formula are rational Hodge maps, and their images are viewed after extension to $\mathbb{C}$. The conclusion persists after passage to a deeper principal level.

Every active spherical character occurring in a theta class of the multiplicity line $P$ has half-modulus projective parameter $$\label{eq:active-satake-triple}
\{q^{1/2},q^{-1/2},e\},$$ where $e=e_0^{-1}$ for a root $e_0^M=\omega$ from Lemma <a href="#lem:scalar-radial-relation" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="lem:scalar-radial-relation">24</a>. It is algebraic and all its complex conjugates have modulus one. For every $\sigma\in\mathop{\mathrm{Gal}}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$, $$\label{eq:active-conjugate-valuations}
\nu_\iota(\sigma e)
 =-\frac32d\bigl((\sigma|_E)^{-1}\bigr)
 =-\frac32v(\sigma|_E).$$ A scalar-conjugate active character has a representative of the form <a href="#eq:active-satake-triple" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:active-satake-triple">[eq:active-satake-triple]</a> with third entry $e'=\pm\sigma e$.
